# Hoda App Operations

## When to Use

- The user sends a .docx of Quran verses / hadiths / martyr wills to import into the Hoda database.
- Working in the `dnh-bit/hoda` repo: DB changes, code merges from user zips, release builds.
- Needing to stop or re-enable the auto Build APK workflow between releases.

Recurring operations for the Hoda Flutter app (repo `github.com/dnh-bit/hoda`, clone at `/data/workspace/hoda`). The user curates Persian religious content in .docx documents and sends them via Telegram; the agent imports them into `assets/hoda.db` and manages builds/releases.

## Ground rules (user-mandated)

- **Never build or release without an explicit order.** Batch small fixes; commits/pushes are fine.
- **Do not ask confirmation questions mid-task.** Once the user delegates an import («اینم از X، بیاور داخل دیتابیس»), execute the whole pipeline and report when done. Asking «should I proceed?» is a correction trigger.
- **Use only the tables in the docx for row data** («کاری به متون متفرقه نداشته باش») — ignore prose/analysis paragraphs as content. Nuance (learned the hard way): prose *section headings* and explicit «حدیث شماره N» anchors are legitimate metadata when the tables lack a field — they were the only source for hadith narrator (گوینده) attribution across the 14 Ma'sumin sections.
- **User-facing buttons must be self-explanatory**: label icon-only buttons with short Persian text next to the icon (user: «کنار اون دکمه ریز بنویس … تا متوجه شوند»). Icon + text label > icon alone, even for compact header actions.
- **PROMPT vs IMPLEMENT is decided by his words, not by the task's nature.** (2026-09-04: he asked for a fix prompt for his external AI; I implemented 0.1.9 directly and he corrected me: «گفتم پرامپت بده تا خودم برم درستش کنم و بیام نه اینکه خودت اصلاح کنه».) Distinguish: he sends a screenshot+fix list and says «اصلاح کن» → implement. He says «پرامپت بده / میخوام خودم با اون هوش مصنوعی حل کنم» → write a precise, file-and-line-level prompt (what to change, what NOT to touch: versionCode formula, `android/`, `.github/`, DB, notification/repository services) and stop. If he later changes his mind («بیخیال… خودت که اصلاحش کردی خوبه»), implement then.
- **A screenshot fix means: change only what's circled + what the numbered list says. Never over- or under-apply.** Two overreach modes both drew corrections on the same release (0.1.9): (a) dropping the circular logo when only the white title text was to be swapped («لوگو حذف نشه»), and (b) deleting the «(−۱)» from the dhikr pill label when only the minus ICON was to be replaced with the undo icon («علامت سمت راستش رو عوض میکردی» — the label had to go back to «اصلاح (−۱)»). Rule of thumb: icon swaps ≠ label edits ≠ removals; each is a separate instruction needing its own mention. State per-element disposition in the report.
- **Rebuilding "the same version" after a correction:** keep versionCode per the digit-concat rule (19 stays 19), bump only the pubspec build number (+19→+20→+21) to force fresh CI; each bump mints a new same-titled release → delete the superseded one and re-apply the Persian changelog. Also re-check the changelog TEXT against the final state each time: the first 0.1.9 body said «نشان الله در نوار بالا» though the artwork was the «هُدا» wordmark (misread calligraphy) — he caught it from the release page. Ambiguous calligraphy must be confirmed with the user before being described anywhere. **He later rejected the +20/+21 scheme entirely** («نسخه رو گذاشتی ۲۱ درصورتیکه طبق فرمولی که بهت گفته بودم باید ۱۹ باشه چون نسخه 0.1.9 هست»): the pubspec build number must EQUAL the digit-concat code (0.1.9+19, never 0.1.9+21) — to force a rebuild at the same version keep the same `+N`; tag reuse mints a fresh release anyway, then delete the stale one.

## Content docx → database import

1. `pip install python-docx` (frequently missing on fresh environments).
2. Extract `doc.tables` only. Every table repeats the header row (`['ردیف', 'متن عربی', ...]`) — skip row 0 of each table and concatenate all data rows in order. Tables can also re-emit the header MID-table (the nahj 101–200 doc repeated its «شماره حکمت» header 3× inside the data) — so skip ANY row whose ردیف cell doesn't parse as a Persian/Latin integer, not just row 0. Tables may be split mid-dataset (e.g. 100 hikam across 5 tables of 20; 120 wills across 2 tables of 60/46): concatenating all tables' data rows in document order restores the full 1..N sequence.
3. Validate before writing: the ردیف column must be exactly 1..N (no gaps, no dupes), every required cell non-empty, row count matches the number the user announced (e.g. «۱۰۰ آیه», «۱۲۰ حدیث»).
4. Persian-text pitfalls (each has bitten this session):
   - Digits are Persian: `s.translate(str.maketrans('۰۱۲۳۴۵۶۷۸۹','0123456789'))` before any `int()` or `\d+` regex.
   - ZWNJ (`\u200c`) inside names («آل‌عمران») breaks exact-match lookups — strip it before map lookups and keep name variants in the surah map (see `references/persian-content-parsing.md`).
   - Source strings like «سوره زمر، آیه ۵۳» / «آیات ۱۵۵ و ۱۵۶» / «آیات ۲۵ تا ۳۰»: split on the Persian comma `،` (U+060C, not ASCII), match `آی\S*\s*(.+)` (covers آیه/آیات), first/last number = ayah range.
   - A regex on «آی\S*» still failed until the source was split on `،` first — parse `part1` (سوره name) and `part2` (ayah numbers) separately.
   - Verses also need a surah NUMBER: keep a 114-entry Persian→Arabic surah-name map (strip ZWNJ before lookup; keep variants like انشراح→الشرح, توحید→الإخلاص) and store `index+1`. Multi-ayah refs («آیات ۲۳ و ۲۴», «آیات ۱ تا ۳») store the FIRST ayah in `ayah` and the full ref string in both `ref` and `source` (legacy format uses Latin digits, e.g. «سوره طه، آیه 25»). Proofread map hits — one doc had a «شوری» typo for شورا.
5. Dedupe against existing rows using **normalized Arabic**: strip harakat `\u064B-\u0652`, `\u0670`, tatweel `\u0640`, unify `أإآٱ→ا`, `ي→ی`, `ك→ک`, collapse whitespace. Skip dupes, report each one, and verify `inserted + duplicates == announced total`. Dedupe key is normalized-Arabic **plus** the Persian translation: the same Arabic text re-used with a different translation/source is a distinct numbered entry and must be KEPT, not merged — a pure-Arabic dedupe once silently dropped one of the 120 hadiths (DB sat at 119 for weeks) until a follow-up batch restored it. For **incremental batches** where the doc repeats old rows plus new ones («۱۲۰ قبلی هم داخلش هست ولی ۱۹۳ جدید اضافه شده»): find the ردیف boundary (old 1..120, new 121..N), dedupe-check the new range against the DB first (expect 0 hits), then insert only the new rows so old ids are untouched. Strip footnote digits glued to tag cells («نفع‌رسانی1» → «نفع‌رسانی») before mapping. For new tags, seed `family` from the tag→family mappings learned in prior batches and hand-review every assignment the seed doesn't cover — an automatic keyword pass misfires on ~1/3 of abstract tags (e.g. «رهایی از استرس» belongs to آرامش و سلامت روان, not اخلاق و کردار). When the user forbids builds/version bumps for the batch («تا زمانیکه نگفتم بیلد نمی‌کنی و نسخه دیتابیس رو ارتقا نمیدی»): commit + push normally, cancel the auto-triggered Build APK run via the API, and leave `dbContentVersion` unchanged. Note: only dedupe when the user wants old rows kept — several imports came with an explicit «قبلی‌ها رو پاک کن» order (see step 6). A «new N..M» section can itself be full of duplicates: the nahj 101–200 doc had 17/100 rows duplicating existing content (8 matching old rows 66–87, 9 repeating inside the new section — 127/152/199 were all حکمت 67). Report the skipped numbers explicitly and leave the numbering gaps; never backfill them with duplicate text just to make 1..N contiguous (dupes would recur in the daily rotation). Re-running the same insert must be a provable no-op (added: 0) — use that as the idempotency check. **Nahj hikam numbers are edition-dependent — never trust the docx's numbering.** The DB follows صبحی صالح («دشتی») numbering, but user docx files compiled by external AIs mix in other editions' numbers plus plain mislabels: the 101–200 doc put الناس أعداء at 113 (true 172), من نصب نفسه at 102 (true 73), and 17/100 rows duplicated existing content — and the original 1–100 import itself carried scattered misnumberings (العفاف at 67 not 68, المرء مخبوء at 76 not 148). For ANY nahj batch, verify every row's number against web ground truth and reconcile the WHOLE table (renumber + dedupe + fill gaps), not just the new range. Full procedure: `references/nahj-number-verification.md`.
6. Follow the user's **replacement/grouping instructions** exactly when given: «مثل قبلی‌ها کاری نداشته باش، فقط جدیدها بمونن» means `DELETE` the pre-existing rows of that table (by id range or count) after inserting, then `VACUUM`. «به همه یه چشم ببین / تفکیک نکن» means ignore any regional/category split in the source doc (e.g. martyr wills split by استان vs ملی) and insert as one flat set in document order.
7. Schema changes must be **additive**: `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN` with nullable TEXT. The app queries whole rows, so old code ignores new columns. Added so far: `hadiths.tag` (topic label), `martyrs.notif_excerpt` (short will excerpt for notifications — the app's `DailyContent.notifPersian` prefers it for notification bodies, falls back to full text), `nahj_wisdoms.tafsir` («مفهوم (تفسیر)» — rendered behind a collapsible «مفهوم و تفسیر» button on the detail screen, gated by `DailyContent.hasTafsir`).
8. After bulk `DELETE`s run `VACUUM`; always finish with `PRAGMA integrity_check`, final per-table counts, and one user-named example row. Back up to `/tmp/hoda_backup_before_<change>.db` first.
9. **Data cleaning the user asks for after an import** is normal follow-up work, not an import failure. Examples from one session: stripping trailing citation numbers glued to the final sentence (pattern: digits immediately before the final `.` or at end-of-string, e.g. «…بدانید10.» → «…بدانید.» — loop the regex until stable because several can trail); deleting superseded rows («۴۰ تای قبلی رو پاک کن»). **Distinguish real content from artifacts before mass-deleting**: «لشکر ۴۱ ثارالله», «گردان ۴۱۹ محرم», «۳۸ سال ویلچرنشینی» are genuine text — only numbers at string-end before terminal punctuation are citation artifacts. Scan for glued digit patterns mid-text and eyeball each context before deciding.

## Rebuild-in-place when a release must be replaced without version change («جایگزین فعلی (0.2.0) کنی», 2026-09-11)

The 0.2.0 pipeline was frozen by the user (build code 20 fixed, no version bump allowed), but content/UI fixes were still ordered. Working pattern: commit content/UI fixes to `main` with the pubspec version UNCHANGED, let the auto Build APK run complete (this is a release-ordered build — do NOT cancel), then swap the APK into the existing release: `gh release upload v0.2.0+20 <apk> --clobber` (or the equivalent API `POST uploads.github.com/.../releases/{id}/assets?name=...` after deleting the old asset). The workflow's `gh release create` step tolerates an existing tag/release when the upload path runs. Also fine (same outcome): `gh release delete-asset` + upload, or `gh run watch` then `gh release upload --clobber v0.2.0+20 hoda-v0.2.0.apk`. Verify the swap took by comparing the release asset's `updated_at` timestamp and re-downloading to re-verify versionCode/cert/DB content.

Same-version pitfalls: users who already installed the replaced build get **no update prompt** (same versionCode) — tell the user explicitly that the APK file changed but in-app version stays 0.2.0, so reviewers must sideload manually; Play Protect treats the new binary as a new file (new appeal form with the new file SHA256).

## Source-doc feature columns → app UI

User docx tables sometimes carry a column with no DB equivalent that they then want visible in the app («یه دکمه مخصوص براش بذار»). Pattern that worked twice: add a nullable TEXT column → map it in `ContentRepository.*From()` into a new optional `DailyContent` field → add the UI hook in the consuming screen (notifs: prefer short excerpt; detail card: collapsible panel gated by a `hasX` getter). Keep the fallback path intact so rows without the column still render.

## Notifications: never arm a repeating notification (0.2.1 double-fire)

`NotificationService._armSchedule` pre-arms `horizonDays` (30) **one-shot** notifications per schedule so each day carries that day's own card with the app closed. The 0.2.1 build additionally armed the horizon's last day with `matchDateTimeComponents: DateTimeComponents.time` as a "never go silent" safety net — and every configured hour delivered **two** notifications instead.

Root cause, read straight out of the shipped package (`/tmp/pub-cache/hosted/pub.dev/flutter_local_notifications-17.2.4/.../FlutterLocalNotificationsPlugin.java`, `zonedSchedule`): when `matchDateTimeComponents != null` the plugin **overwrites `scheduledDateTime`** with `getNextFireDateMatchingDateTimeComponents()`, which builds *today's date at the requested time* and loops `plusDays(1)` while it is before now. The requested date (day 13) is discarded, so the "day 13" alarm landed on the exact same instant as day 0's one-shot and then re-fired daily from the receiver — two alarms, two notifications, forever.

Rules: (a) never pass `matchDateTimeComponents` to `zonedSchedule` — there is no way to schedule "daily starting N days from now" with this plugin, so a daily repeat can never sit behind a window of one-shots without doubling it; (b) the pre-armed window is renewed by every app start (`restoreSchedule`), and past the horizon notifications pause until the next launch — the release note must say so honestly; (c) a regression guard lives in `test/widget_test.dart` (source-scans for `matchDateTimeComponents:` outside comments) plus `horizonDays < _idsPerSchedule` (50) or two slots would share an id. Note `flutter analyze`/`flutter test` **cannot run on this box** (953 MB cgroup kills the analysis server and the Dart frontend compiler: "the Dart compiler exited unexpectedly") — `dart format` still parses for syntax, and CI's `flutter build apk` is the real compile check.

## Daily-content rotation (no-repeat)

The home screen's «آیه/حدیث/حکمت/وصیت روز» cards and the daily notifications pick from a **deterministic no-repeat deck**, not a simple day-of-year index: per-table shuffled id list (Fisher–Yates seeded by an xorshift32 from a stable table-name hash, or a fresh random seed) + an epoch counter that advances once per calendar day, persisted in the bundled-but-previously-unused `shuffle_state` table. Each item shows exactly once per full cycle. The home screen's «تغییر محتوای امروز» button (reload icon + text label + spinner while working) calls `getDailyContentShuffled()` → advances the epoch, re-shuffles, then **re-arms `NotificationService.restoreSchedule()`** so scheduled notifications match the new picks. User-confirmed expectation: daily content must visibly change at midnight AND on demand, never repeat until the whole deck cycles. Simulate the algorithm in Python (100 items × 100 days → exactly 100 distinct picks) before shipping changes to it.

## Weekday-keyed content looks "one day off" — find WHICH column drifted

The `zekr` table (one row per weekday: `day`/`day_key` + `zekr_arabic`/`zekr_farsi` + `amal`/`note`) once showed every day shifted by one (Wednesday displayed Thursday's zekr). The first hypothesis — labels are wrong — was WRONG: rotating `day`/`day_key` broke `amal`/`note`, which consistently referenced the ORIGINAL labels (Thursday row mentions Thursday ghusl and Friday-eve rituals, Friday row mentions Friday ghusl and Komayl/Nodbeh). Rule: **before rotating anything, cross-check the day references inside the OTHER columns** — the column family that agrees with the labels is correct, the odd one out drifted. Here `zekr_arabic`/`zekr_farsi` were the shifted columns, so each row took the previous row's zekr text and the labels stayed put. Verify all 7 days end-to-end (normalized day-name match + exactly one row per day + expected stem per day) before committing.

**Verification-script pitfall:** assertion literals hand-typed in the terminal routinely contain Persian ی (U+06CC) / ک (U+06A9) while the DB's Arabic text uses Arabic ي (U+064A) / ة — equality fails on bytes that look identical on screen, and the failure misleads you into doubting correct data (this burned three check runs in a row). When a check fails on a visually-identical string, hex-dump both sides (`[hex(ord(c)) for c in s]`); prefer stems that avoid the ambiguous glyphs, or pull the expected strings out of the DB itself.

## Prerelease versions + same-beta rebuilds

`build.yml` publishes any version whose name contains `-` (e.g. `0.2.0-beta`) as a GitHub **pre-release** (`case "$TITLE" in *-*) PRERELEASE_FLAG="--prerelease"` before `gh release create`); plain versions stay full releases and keep the «Latest» badge. When the user orders a corrected build **in place of** the previous beta («به جای نسخه قبلی بزار، قبلی رو حذف کن»): `DELETE` the release AND its tag, then `POST /actions/workflows/build.yml/dispatches {"ref":"main"}` (no version bump — same tag is recreated), then verify the new asset (size/SHA, `APK Sig Block 42` bytes, versionName, and the bundled DB at `assets/flutter_assets/assets/hoda.db` queried with sqlite3). Added 2026-09-08 for the 0.2.0-beta zekr-fix rebuild.

## Build/release gating with GitHub Actions

Every push to `main` auto-triggers the **Build APK** workflow. When pushing DB/code changes while builds are forbidden:

1. `POST /repos/dnh-bit/hoda/actions/runs/{run_id}/cancel` → 202 on the auto-run (get run_id from `GET .../actions/runs?per_page=1`).
2. `PUT /repos/dnh-bit/hoda/actions/workflows/{workflow_id}/disable` → 204 (workflow_id from `GET .../actions/workflows`, name "Build APK"). Disable immediately after any build-forbidden push — the user will not tolerate surprise builds (an auto-run triggered by a DB push was cancelled this way).
3. When the user later orders a release: `PUT .../enable`, bump `pubspec.yaml` version, push, watch the run to `completed success`, then verify release title = plain version and asset `hoda-v<short>.apk`. If the first build fails, **pull the job logs** (`GET .../actions/jobs/{job_id}/logs` — grep for `Error:`) and fix-forward with a follow-up commit; the workflow re-triggers automatically on push. Only one step ever failed so far: `TextButton.icon` has **no `tooltip` param** (that is `IconButton`-only) — a param error in one widget fails the whole `kernel_snapshot` step. Balance-checking braces locally does NOT catch wrong named parameters; only a real compile does.

Releases use stable upload signing via repo secrets (`HODA_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_B64`, `HODA_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD`) so updates install in-place; release titles are plain version numbers, assets version-named `hoda-vX.Y.Z.apk`. Verify the shipped APK's signature after each release: download it, `unzip META-INF/CERT.RSA`, `keytool -printcert` → must show `CN=Hoda App`.

## Bundled-database versioning (upgrade installs)

The app copies `assets/hoda.db` to app storage **only when missing** — users who update the APK keep their stale DB copy and never see new content/schema. `DatabaseHelper` carries `dbContentVersion` (currently **4**) + a SharedPreferences stamp: on init, if the stored stamp is older, the bundled file is re-copied over. **Bump `dbContentVersion` on EVERY release that ships a changed bundled DB — including data-only fixes (cleaned text, new column values), not just schema changes.** The stamp was left at 2 through the 0.1.0→0.1.1→0.1.2 releases, so upgraded installs holding stamp 2 silently skipped the re-copy and users kept reporting the pre-fix data (trailing citation numbers, missing narrator) even though the shipped APK's embedded DB was verified clean — the classic symptom is «APK is fine but the app shows old data» → suspect a stale local copy skipped by the stamp. Debug by unzipping the shipped APK's `assets/flutter_assets/assets/hoda.db` and querying it directly. **Exception (user-ordered, 2026-09-08): when the bad DB never shipped anywhere** (fix still unreleased, no installs carry it), KEEP the stamp unchanged — he re-downloads the file himself and a bump is churn. Only bump once a build carrying the fix is actually ordered. Corollary learned 2026-09-11: a content commit shipped inside release A **without** a bump leaves every upgrader from earlier releases holding the stale copy; the NEXT ordered release (B) must then carry the bump (4→5 covered the 17-gap nahj fill), or those users never see the content even though the APK's embedded DB is correct. Since then the rule of thumb: any release whose bundled DB differs from the previous release's bundled DB → bump `dbContentVersion` in that release's commit.

## Version & store metadata (Cafe Bazaar etc.)

App stores order updates by **versionCode** (an integer), not versionName. Flutter maps pubspec `version: X.Y.Z+N` to `versionCode = N`, which produced arbitrary codes (0.0.9→1, 0.1.2→6) — Bazaar displayed «version 6». Fixed permanently in `android/app/build.gradle`: versionCode is recomputed from versionName by **digit concatenation** (user's explicit choice): `0.1.2 → 012 (12)`, `0.1.3 → 013`, `0.2.0 → 020`, `1.0.0 → 100`. The pubspec build number is now ignored by gradle. Pitfalls:

- The workflow tag is `vX.Y.Z+N`, so **changing N creates a second release with the same title** — delete superseded releases via `DELETE /repos/.../releases/{id}` so only one «X.Y.Z» remains.
- Verify the shipped APK's versionCode after changing the scheme: `pip install androguard==3.3.5` → `AXMLPrinter(manifest).get_xml()` → regex `versionCode="..."`. (A fresh download is required — the GitHub release asset `updated_at` timestamp tells you whether the clobber actually replaced the file.)
- Known collision far off but real: `0.10.0` and `1.0.0` both → code 100. Revisit the scheme before either milestone.
- **Prerelease→stable promotion (0.1.5-beta → 0.1.5, 2026-09-01):** the user decided the collision is acceptable while the app is not on a store: keep pure digit concat, versionCode stays 15 for both beta+15 and stable+15, no `max()` blending. A `Math.max(concat, buildNumber)` fix was tried and **explicitly reverted on user order** («ورژن کد رو بزار همون ۱۵، نزار ۱۹، اشکالی ایجاد نمیکنه») — do not reintroduce build-number blending. Two CI pitfalls hit during the attempt: there is **no `flutter.buildNumber` extension property** in `android/app/build.gradle` (only `flutter.versionName` and `flutter.versionCode` exist — the latter IS the pubspec build number; referencing `flutter.buildNumber` fails gradle), and gradle DSL errors surface only in the Actions log (`assembleRelease failed`), not locally.

**In-app version label**: `kHodaVersionFa` in `settings_screen.dart` is a hand-synced const (user-reported stale twice: showed ۰.۰.۹ during 0.1.x, then ۰.۱.۲ during the 0.1.3 release). **Always set it to the version being released, not the previous one**, and a guard test in `widget_test.dart` compares it against pubspec so a stale const fails CI.

## Default notifications on master-enable

Tapping the main notifications switch in settings seeds four enabled schedules (07:00 آیه روز، 10:00 حدیث روز، 13:00 حکمت روز، 16:00 وصیت شهید) — the user's explicit design: «به جای اینکه دونه یه دونه اعلان بسازه». Fresh-setup detection must treat **both** an empty schedule list *and* the untouched legacy-migration placeholder (single 08:00 `random` schedule that `_migrateLegacy` creates on first run) as «fresh»; only a genuinely user-configured list is respected untouched.

## Home hero quick-settings button

The home-screen hero chip is now a tappable «تنظیم اعلان» button (`Material`+`InkWell`, `Icons.notifications_active_outlined`) wired through `HomeTab.onOpenSettings` → `_openSettings`. Pattern: convert decorative chips into real buttons only when the user asks for a visible entry point — icon + Persian label, matching the self-explanatory-buttons rule above.

## Merging external-AI source zips (user's Claude/other-AI passes)

The user periodically sends a full source zip edited by other AIs («با کمک سایر AI ها ...», or routed through him with the AI's own delivery notes). Merge protocol that worked (0.1.5 onboarding, 0.1.6 UI refresh, 0.1.5-modern-ui + 0.2.0-ui-redesign samples):

1. Unzip to `/tmp/hoda_vXXX` and **inventory** (`find . -type f`, exclude `.git/`, `build/`, `.dart_tool/`) — zips often carry extra payload (raw scrape corpora `content/`, `assets/content/*.json`, helper shell scripts) beyond the code change. Zips may wrap everything in a version-named subfolder (`hoda-0.2.0-16/…`) — find the inner root first.
2. **md5-diff the zip against the repo** file-by-file (walk both trees, skip the junk dirs + `pubspec.lock`). Never `cp -r` the zip over the repo — diffs range from ONE file (0.1.6) to 33 (0.2.0: 21 modified + 12 new), and wholesale copying risks clobbering `.github/`, signing config, or CI-critical files the AI pass didn't have.
3. **Integrity gate before accepting** (external passes are presentation-layer by contract): md5 `assets/hoda.db`, and `diff` `AndroidManifest.xml`, `android/app/build.gradle`, `lib/services/database_helper.dart`, `lib/services/notification_service.dart`, `lib/services/content_repository.dart`, and the pubspec `dependencies:` block against the repo. ALL must be byte-identical / dep-identical; any deviation is a red flag to inspect or reject. Also re-grep the shipped features the user cares about (family chips, onboarding notif activation, Jalali chip, 4-default seeding) inside the merged tree — a UI pass has deleted working features before.
   **Stale-base zips are the normal case, not the exception (0.2.1, 2026-09-30).** The zip's root folder name and its `git log` show which commit it forked; a fork off the RELEASE commit misses every later fix, so files it "changes" may be REVERTS of work the user ordered after that commit. 0.2.1 rejected 3 of 8 differing files this way: `assets/hoda.db` (stale pre-enrichment استغفار rows), `salawat_screen.dart` (undid the user's own button wording), `docs/play_protect_appeal.md` (dropped the 0.2.0 entry). Always diff the zip's "changes" against `git log` before copying — a diff is not automatically an improvement. Note that files which ARE the release's subject (`notification_service.dart`, `database_helper.dart`) legitimately differ; accept those but check they didn't drag the reverted neighbours along.
4. Static pre-checks (the external AIs often can't run `flutter analyze` and say so): resolve every relative `import`/`part` target exists, comment-aware brace-balance every `.dart` file. Expect unused-import warnings to survive; only a real compile catches wrong named params. The AI's chat-transcript claims may also be loose — verify features in the actual code (e.g. it claimed "bookmarks that actually work" and shipped a real `favorites_store.dart`, fine; but check any claimed DB/counter behavior in code before trusting it).
5. Read the changed file(s) fully and sanity-check referenced APIs exist in the repo model (`hasArabic/hasPersian/hasSource`, etc.).
6. Copy only the real diffs (+ new files), bump version if releasing, sync `kHodaVersionFa` in the same commit, push, watch CI. A `UI_CHANGES.md`-style report inside the zip is the external AI's summary — commit it for provenance. If the AI's delivery notes mention caveats («couldn't run analyze»), treat the first CI build as the real analysis and fix forward.
7. **User artwork can ride along with a sample request** — the user may send an image (e.g. gold «الله» calligraphy) to be embedded as a brand asset. Convert white-background JPG → transparent PNG (PIL bounding-box crop + near-white→alpha mapping, verify corners/ratio by reading pixels), register in `pubspec.yaml` assets, wrap in a small widget with an `errorBuilder` text fallback, and confirm via `unzip -l` that it's inside the built APK. See «User-marked screenshot fixes & brand-image assets» below.

## Pre-review test builds (user-ordered pipeline, 2026-09-03)

The user sends UI samples edited by other AIs and wants them **built but NOT released** — delivered in chat for review, published only after his explicit approval («نمیخوام همشون ریلز بشن … واسم بیلدشون کنی و همینجا نسخه اش رو برام بفرستی و زمانی که تایید دادم، تو گیت هاب نمایش بدی»). Pipeline:

1. Overlay **only the md5-diffed files** onto a `test/<sample-name>` branch — `main` stays untouched. Don't bump the version for samples; test builds never ship. (Exception: if the external AI already set the sample's own version in pubspec + `kHodaVersionFa`, keep it — it distinguishes samples on the phone's app-info screen; versionCode collisions between samples are harmless pre-store.)
2. `.github/workflows/build-test.yml` builds on `push: branches-ignore: [main]` (+ `workflow_dispatch`) → uploads a **private artifact** `hoda-test-apk` (7-day retention). No release, no tag, invisible on the public Releases page. Its `key.properties` must use `keyAlias=hoda-upload` — a typo'd alias fails `packageRelease` with `Failed to read key … No key with alias '<alias>' found in keystore` (cost one build cycle). The fix landed on `main`, but **merging `main` into a test branch only helps after main actually has the fix** — verify with `git log --all --oneline | grep <fix>` + `git branch --contains <sha>` before pushing the merge; a no-op merge ("Already up to date") that still lacks the fix means the fix never reached main (cherry-pick/merge errors surface only in terminal output — always read them).
3. Even a workflow-file-only push to `main` triggers the release **Build APK** run — cancel it (202) like any build-forbidden push.
4. Deliver the test APK in chat. **Telegram bot upload fails for APKs >50MB** — a 52.5MB APK never arrived and the user had to ask «بفرست، اگه نمیتونی بفرستی لینک دانلود از گیت هاب بده». Working fallback that the user **approved** after the draft-404 episode: publish the sample as a **prerelease on a `test-<name>` tag** (`POST /releases` with `draft:false, prerelease:true`, then upload the asset via `POST uploads.github.com/repos/.../releases/{id}/assets?name=…` with binary body + `Content-Type: application/vnd.android.package-archive`). It stays off the «Latest» badge (Latest tracks the newest full release), is effectively invisible to casual repo visitors, but its `browser_download_url` works **anonymously** — no 404. User: «موقت بزار تو صفحه عمومی فوقش بعدا حذف یا تغییرش میدیم». Caveats learned: draft releases are useless here (their asset URLs 404 even with a token via the browser route; only the API `GET /releases/assets/{id}` + `Accept: application/octet-stream` route works), and a freshly-published prerelease's anonymous download can 404 for ~1 minute before propagating — verify with `curl -w "%{http_code}"` + size check before sending the link. Delete the prerelease when the sample is approved or rejected.
5. **Approval outcomes (real pattern, 2026-09-04):** the user reviewed two samples at once and assigned each a different disposition — expect this, it is not always «approve the one». The actual order: UI Redesign → official **0.1.8**; Modern UI → official **0.1.7-beta**; the then-current 0.1.6 stable → **relabeled 0.1.6-beta** (its stable release deleted). Protocol per disposition:
   - **Official stable:** merge `test/*` → `main`, set the version the USER dictates, push, watch CI, then write full Persian release notes into the GitHub release body (user explicitly asked «همراه با متن تغییرات در گیت هاب»).
   - **Official beta:** same merge, `version: X.Y.Z-beta+N`, `kHodaVersionFa = 'X.Y.Z بتا'`, then PATCH the release `prerelease: true` — the workflow publishes betas as full releases, which would steal the «Latest» badge from the stable.
   - **Relabel current as beta:** rename version + label on main, push, delete the superseded stable release.
   - Delete every `test-*` sample prerelease once decisions land.
6. **Version renumbering is the user's call, not the zip's:** the ui-redesign zip shipped itself as `0.2.0+16`; the user said «به عنوان نسخه 0.1.8 رسمی منتشر کن». Treat the sample's pubspec version as advisory only — always confirm/override at promotion time and resync `kHodaVersionFa`.
7. **Merging a second full-rebuild sample into a main that already contains the first one conflicts massively** (12 UU files when ui-redesign hit modern-ui's main — both rebuilt the same presentation layer from the same base). Working resolution: `git checkout --theirs` for ALL conflicted UI files (the approved sample wins wholesale), then hand-fix the version files (`pubspec.yaml`, `settings_screen.dart` — the settings conflict left BOTH `kHodaVersionFa` consts in the file, a guaranteed duplicate-const compile error), and `grep -rn '^<<<<<<<'` the tree before committing. A conflicted merge does NOT fail the commit — git committed it with the duplicate const inside; only CI would have caught it.

## User-marked screenshot fixes & brand-image assets (v0.1.9 pattern)

The user sends app screenshots with **red circles around problem spots** + a short numbered fix list («دورش قرمز کردم … عوض بشه») and expects the fixes implemented directly as a patch release — not routed back to the external AI that wrote the UI (but see the prompt-vs-implement ground rule above: if he asks for a prompt instead, write the prompt). Cycle: `vision_analyze` both screenshots (the app shot for locating the marked elements, the artwork shot for asset quality) → implement → bump patch version (0.1.8→0.1.9) → release with a short Persian changelog in the GitHub body. **Rebuilding the same version after a correction:** keep versionCode per the digit-concat rule, bump only the pubspec build number (`0.1.9+19 → 0.1.9+20 → 0.1.9+21`) to force a fresh CI build — the changed tag creates a second release with the same title, so delete the superseded one and re-apply the Persian changelog body to the surviving release (and fix the changelog text itself if the correction was about wording — one 0.1.9 body mislabeled the wordmark as «الله»).

**Fine-grained visual nudge pattern** (0.1.9, «باید کمی جابه‌جا بشه و بیاد پایین‌تر»): when a header group (logo + wordmark) renders top-heavy inside an AppBar, wrap the `title:` widget in `Padding(EdgeInsets.only(top: N))` (N≈9 worked) rather than resizing assets — the AppBar centers the whole widget, so padding shifts it down without touching asset scales.

**Icon-vs-label surgical swap** (0.1.9 final state): the dhikr «اصلاح (−۱)» pill = `Icons.undo_rounded` (replaced `Icons.remove`) + unchanged Persian label text including «(−۱)». When the user says «علامت سمت راستش رو عوض کن», that is the ICON only — never normalize the label «for consistency».

**Turning user-supplied raster art into an app asset** (worked for the gold «هُدا» calligraphy replacing the app-bar «هُدا» white text; NOTE — the artwork is the brand wordmark, not «الله» as first assumed):

1. The artwork usually arrives as JPG on a **solid white background** — a white box on the dark-green app bar looks broken. Convert to transparent PNG with PIL: threshold-scan for the non-white bounding box → crop with a small margin → map near-white pixels to alpha 0 (and the 225–240 halo band to partial alpha ~110) so no fringe survives.
2. **Verify transparency by reading pixel data, not by trusting a vision model** — the vision analyzer described the processed PNG as "still has a white box" while PIL showed `mode: RGBA`, corner alpha=0, ~80% transparent. Check corners + a content pixel + transparent-pixel ratio before shipping.
3. Register the PNG in `pubspec.yaml` `assets:` (a new asset file without registration is invisible at runtime), reference it through a tiny dedicated widget (`lib/widgets/hoda_wordmark.dart`, `Image.asset` + `errorBuilder` fallback to the old text), then **verify it landed inside the built APK**: `unzip -l app.apk | grep <asset-name>`.
4. Scope discipline: the user circled the *app-bar title* only — the settings hero's circular logo + name block stayed untouched. Fix exactly what's circled — and when a fix *replaces* one element of a composite (here: white title text next to the circular logo), preserve the neighbours: the 0.1.9 first pass dropped the circular logo from the title row and had to be re-released (+20) with logo restored. When rendering user artwork whose subject is ambiguous (a calligraphy that could read as «الله» or «هُدا»), confirm the reading with the user before describing it in the report — I misidentified the gold typography as «الله» when it was the brand wordmark «هُدا», and the wrong description misdirected the fix AND leaked into the published GitHub release notes (he caught it there: «متن توی توضیحات نسخه اشتباهه»).

**Final 0.1.9 app-bar anatomy** (after two correction rounds, +21): `title:` = `Padding(top: 9)` → `Row[HodaLogo(size: 30), SizedBox(width: 8), HodaWordmark(height: 34)]` — circular logo KEPT, gold «هُدا» typography replaces only the white text, whole group nudged down 9dp. Salawat pill: `InfoPill(label: 'اصلاح (−۱)', icon: Icons.undo_rounded)` — icon swapped, label preserved verbatim.

